  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1114 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nAside from a couple pop up storms earlier, it had been pretty \nquiet across the region as the well-advertised cold front \ncontinued its southward progress across the area. That said, a \nfew storms had started to pop up in vicinity of the front in \nsouthwest Iowa as of 1030 PM. With the front quickly scouring \nout instability, these should be fairly short-lived, but a few \ncould produce some small hail or gusty winds through 1-2 AM. \nGuidance then suggests we'll see more development well behind \nthe front after midnight as some weak shortwave energy clips the\narea and we see a little strengthening frontogenesis as \nnortherly low level flow ramps up. However, with lack of \ninstability, not expecting any severe weather with these, and \neven lightning strikes may be few and far between.\n\nThat precip should exit south/dissipate toward 9-10 AM and give\nway to a warm, but much more \"manageable\" day, with highs in \nthe mid 80s to around 90 and a nice north breeze. The HRRR also \ncontinues to show smoke moving in behind the front, but keeps \nnear-surface concentrations rather low, with upstream \nobservations currently showing no visibility reductions, so \nthinking it'll manifest mainly as some milky looking skies.\n\nWe'll be much cooler Wednesday through Friday, as the upper \nlevel ridge axis gets pushed well back to our west and a \nsecondary cold front passes through. Highs will fall into the \nmid 70s to lower 80s all three days. In addition, we'll have \nseveral chances for more widespread showers and storms as \nvarious shortwaves push through the area. The first now looks to\narrive Wednesday afternoon with a second stronger and more \npersistent stream of shortwave energy arriving early Thursday \nand lingering into early Friday. Chances are generally in the \n40-80% range at times through this period, with the highest \nbeing mostly in southeast NE, where consensus paints a 30- 50% \nchance of at least 1\" of rain. Definitely still some timing \nkinks to work out as we get closer, as there will likely be some\noccasional breaks in the rain and people will certainly want to\nget out and enjoy the temperatures in the 70s.\n\nUnfortunately, those nicer temperatures will be short-lived as \nthe upper level ridge axis pushes back eastward this weekend. \nExpect highs to climb back into the upper 80s to mid 90s on \nSaturday followed by widespread 90s Sunday into next week. There\ncould also be a couple additional storm chances in there as the\nwarm and humid airmass returns and a few weak shortwaves ripple\nthrough the flow, but still lots of details to work out there. \nAs far as severe weather chances go, various machine learning \nalgorithms generally keep us around or under 5% over the next 7 \ndays.",
